\section{How the tables fit together}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{env}} is the time axis. Every row is one site in one simulated time
step (usually one year), and every site has the same years. The first row
of a site drives the first step, starting from the site's initial trees.
\item \strong{\code{init_cohort}} is the starting state: the trees of every site at the
first census, grouped into cohorts. The number of patches per site is read
from it.
\item \strong{\code{data}} is what the simulation is compared with: for every site,
census year and species, the observed stand variables and demographic
rates. An observation with a given \code{year} is compared with the simulated
state at the end of the step with that \code{year} in \code{env}, so every \code{year} in
\code{data} must also be a \code{year} of \code{env}.
\item \strong{\code{disturbance}} (optional) removes trees in given years.
\item \strong{\code{tree_data}} (optional) holds the individual trees, for the two ways of
scoring growth and mortality per tree (\link{finn_loss}).
}

The sites of \code{data}, \code{env} and \code{init_cohort} must be the same set; the ids
need not be \verb{1..N}. Species are coded \verb{1..N_species} in every table.
\code{\link[=resolveSiteIDs]{resolveSiteIDs()}} turns site names and species names into these
codes and returns the lookup tables.
}

\section{\code{data} -- the observations}{

One row per \code{siteID}, \code{year} and \code{species}. Columns:\tabular{ll}{
   column \tab what it is \cr
   \code{siteID} \tab integer site index \cr
   \code{year} \tab the census year; must be a \code{year} of \code{env} \cr
   \code{species} \tab integer species code \verb{1..N_species} \cr
   \code{dbh} \tab mean diameter at breast height of the living trees of that species, cm \cr
   \code{ba} \tab basal area of the living trees, m2 per patch (the mean over the site's patches) \cr
   \code{trees} \tab number of living trees per patch (the mean over the patches; may be fractional) \cr
   \code{growth} \tab relative diameter growth of the survivors per time step, \code{dbh / dbh_before - 1} (the increment between two censuses when one step is one census interval; for intervals of several steps see \code{finn(growth_rate = )}) \cr
   \code{mort} \tab share of the trees alive at the previous census that died by this one (0 to 1) \cr
   \code{reg} \tab recruits (trees that grew past \code{recruits_dbh}) since the previous census, per hectare \cr
}


\code{dbh}, \code{ba} and \code{trees} describe the stand at the census; \code{growth}, \code{mort}
and \code{reg} describe what happened since the previous census. Optional
columns, filled in by FINN when absent:\tabular{lll}{
   column \tab what it is \tab when absent \cr
   \code{period_length} \tab the number of time steps since the previous census (the interval the rates refer to) \tab one step: each rate is compared with a single simulated step \cr
   \code{n_at_risk} \tab trees alive at the previous census behind \code{mort} \tab 1: every mortality observation weighs the same \cr
   \code{growth_n} \tab trees behind the \code{growth} mean \tab 1: every growth observation weighs the same \cr
}


With \code{period_length}, the simulated growth and mortality of the interval's
steps are averaged and the simulated recruitment is summed before they are
compared with the observation. \code{n_at_risk} and \code{growth_n} weight the
mortality and growth terms by the number of trees behind each observation
(a mortality rate from 100 trees says more than one from a single tree).
\code{\link[=makeObsData]{makeObsData()}} produces all of them. Further optional columns exist
for the size-class and quantile aggregations (\code{n_class_1}, ..., \code{dbh_q10},
...; see \link{finn_loss}).

\strong{Missing values.} Every response column must exist, but any value may be
\code{NA}: a response that was not observed in a cell (site, year, species) is
simply left out of the comparison for that cell, and the other responses
of the cell are still used. A species that does not occur on a site needs
a row with 0 for \code{ba}, \code{trees} and \code{reg} (so that FINN learns it is
absent) and \code{NA} for \code{dbh}, \code{growth} and \code{mort} (there is no tree to
measure). \code{\link[=makeObsData]{makeObsData()}} writes these rows.

\strong{Incomplete series.} Sites need not share census years, and a site may
have fewer censuses than another: a (site, year) with no row is treated as
unobserved in that year, and \code{period_length} may differ between sites and
censuses. When it does, every site is scored over its own interval. A
census with \code{NA} \code{period_length} next to one with a value counts as a
one-step interval on the usual path; on the per-site path (interval
lengths that differ between sites, \code{loss_aggregation = "tree"}) every
window is read from the column and a site without a finite value stops
the fit with a message. Years of \code{env} before a site's first census and
after its last are simulated but not scored.

\strong{Units.} Use the units of your inventory consistently: \code{dbh} in cm,
areas in m2 per patch of \code{patch_size} ha, recruits per hectare. The
per-hectare quantities depend on \code{patch_size}, so pass the plot size of
the inventory to \code{fit(patch_size = )}.

