\section{\code{env} -- the environment of every simulated step}{

One row per \code{siteID} and \code{year}, with one column per predictor and nothing
else: no patch column, no response. Every site must have the same years,
every (site, year) a value of every predictor, and the years must run from
the first step to simulate to the last observation in \code{data}; a year that
is missing or \code{NA} cannot be simulated and stops the fit with a message.
At least one predictor is needed; a process whose \code{formula} names none has
nothing to respond to.

The predictors are given in their natural units. \code{fit(env_autoscale = TRUE)} (the default) standardises every column to mean 0 and standard
deviation 1 and stores the constants on the model, so the same raw table
layout works for \code{\link[=simulateForest]{simulateForest()}} afterwards. For the bundled FIA
data the predictors are the bioclimatic variables of each plot:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{head(fia_env_dt, 3)
#>    siteID  year  temp tempmax tempmin  prec precseas precwarmq
#> 1:      1     0  3.96    24.7   -10.4   547     30.0       101
#> 2:      1     1  3.96    24.7   -10.4   547     30.0       101
#> 3:      1     2  3.96    24.7   -10.4   547     30.0       101
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A climate that does not change between years is repeated for every year,
as here; a year-by-year climate gives every step its own row. Which
columns a process uses is set by \code{createProcess(formula = )}, e.g.
\code{~ temp + prec}; the default \code{~.} uses every predictor column.
}

\section{\code{disturbance} -- optional}{

One row per \code{siteID} and \code{year}, over exactly the sites and years of \code{env},
with the column \code{intensity}: the probability (0 to 1) that a patch of the
site loses all its trees in that year. Every patch is drawn separately, so
an intensity of 0.1 clears on average one patch in ten; use 0 where nothing
happens. \code{NULL} (the default) means no disturbance.
}

\section{\code{init_cohort} -- the initial trees}{

A \code{CohortMat}, the trees of every site at the first census grouped into
cohorts of one species and one diameter per patch, built by
\code{\link[=makeInitCohorts]{makeInitCohorts()}} from a table with \code{siteID}, \code{patchID}, \code{species},
\code{dbh} and optionally \code{trees} (stems the row stands for, default 1).
\code{makeInitCohorts(dbh_binsize = )} bins the diameters into fewer, cheaper
cohorts; without it every tree is a cohort, which the per-tree scoring
needs. The number of patches per site is read from \code{init_cohort}, so
\code{fit(patches = )} is ignored when it is given. Without \code{init_cohort} every
site starts from bare ground with \code{patches} empty patches -- a succession
run, not a calibration.
}

\section{\code{tree_data} -- optional, the individual trees}{

One row per tree and census, needed only to score growth or mortality per
tree. Always: \code{siteID}, \code{year} (the census that closes the tree's
interval, the \code{year} the same interval has in \code{data}), \code{patchID},
\code{species}, \code{growth} (its relative growth rate per time step, \code{NA} if not
observed) and \code{died} (0/1 over the interval, \code{NA} if not observed). For
\code{loss_aggregation = "tree"} also \code{dbh} at the start of the interval, and
optionally \code{trees}; for \code{loss_resolution = "tree"} also \code{cohortID}, the
cohort the tree started as (\code{makeInitCohorts(treeID_table = TRUE)} returns
the table that carries it). \link{finn_loss} explains the two routes.
}

\section{The helper functions}{

\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=makeObsData]{makeObsData()}} turns a tree list (one row per tree and inventory,
with site, patch, year, species name, dbh and status) into the
observation table, per patch or aggregated per site, with the units above.
\item \code{\link[=resolveSiteIDs]{resolveSiteIDs()}} replaces site, patch and species names by the
integer codes, aligns the observation, environment and tree tables on
them, and can build the initial cohorts in the same call.
\item \code{\link[=makeInitCohorts]{makeInitCohorts()}} builds \code{init_cohort} from the trees at the first
census.
\item \code{\link[=climateDF2array]{climateDF2array()}}, \code{\link[=obsDF2arrays]{obsDF2arrays()}} and \code{\link[=CohortMat]{CohortMat()}}
are the lower-level converters behind them.
}
}

